From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 08:07:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 23/28] feat(api): read a frame's HTML over HTTP with an agent
 key
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A script that wants a frame's markup (a build step, a diff against the
app's repo, a review bot) had to speak MCP to call get_frame. GET
/api/frames/:id/html returns the same saved HTML as text/html.

- An agent key (Authorization: Bearer dpk_…) is checked ahead of the
  session gate, the way /mcp checks it: resolved to its account on every
  request, so a revoked key or a banned account stops at once. Any other
  request goes on to the session gate and a session route, so the editor's
  own session can use it too.
- Read-only and view-level: the account's canvas role decides, as in
  get_frame. A frame it cannot open is a 404, so ids do not leak. The key
  reaches no other /api route.
- The body is somebody's design and may carry scripts; served from doop's
  origin it would run with doop's cookies if a browser opened it. So the
  response is CSP sandboxed (opaque origin, default-src 'none'), nosniff,
  no-referrer and private, no-store. X-Doop-Frame-Updated-At tells a
  poller whether the frame changed.

No public URL (as /i/ images have): HTML is the design's source, not a
render, and a link that needs no key would be new exposure.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
